WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:07.319
The Texas Chain Saw Massacre is one of the
most important horror films of all time.

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:13.450
Not only did it inspire a half dozen sequels
and reboot films, it was also a completely

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:18.929
new addition to the horror genre that opened
the floodgates of gore.

00:00:18.929 --> 00:00:24.519
Leatherface is still one of the most unsettling
villains in movie history, and there's a rich

00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:30.970
history behind the first movie in the series
that even the most diehard of Leatherface

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.769
fans might be surprised to learn.

00:00:33.769 --> 00:00:39.370
Keep watching to learn the untold truth of
The Texas Chain Saw Massacre...

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.530
Under the boardwalk

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:46.489
When director Tobe Hooper and writer Kim Henkel
decided to team up again after their previous

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:52.359
collaboration on the low-budget, indie movie
Eggshells, they knew they wanted to do something

00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:54.460
in the horror milieu.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:55.540
But what?

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:01.499
At first, the pair considered a more sinister
version of the already-dark Hansel and Gretel

00:01:01.499 --> 00:01:07.430
fable, but then they looked to another type
of fairy tale narrative: Trolls.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.119
"A 5-6-7-8!"

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:13.120
Well, not those trolls...more like these trolls.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.890
If they'd gone with their original thinking,
the movie would've been called Head Cheese

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.420
and followed a group of trolls living under
a bridge.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.899
It might've been about as successful as their
experimental Eggshells if they hadn't come

00:01:26.899 --> 00:01:29.649
up with a more mainstream concept.

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:34.020
Luckily, they stumbled upon a fatal new idea...

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.799
Ed Gein, and then some

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:44.090
Any Texas Chain Saw fan worth his or her salt
knows that Leatherface was in large part inspired

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:51.020
by real-life criminal, Ed Gein, but he wasn't
the only skin-loving psychopath to lend some

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.200
creepy realism to the story.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:59.520
Hooper told Texas Monthly that the idea for
Leatherface was actually derived from a story

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:06.509
his doctor once told about a med school prank
where he cut off a corpse’s face.

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:11.920
"When he was in pre-med school he skinned
a cadaver's face and dried it out and wore

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.930
it for a mask to a Halloween party.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.220
So I thought 'Ok that's cool.'"

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:22.840
That's not all: Hooper also looked to a Texan
teen serial slasher named Elmer Wayne Henley.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:23.840
Hooper explained:

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.320
"He kind of puffed out his chest and said,
'I did these crimes, and I'm gonna stand up

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.390
and take it like a man.'

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:36.520
Well, that struck me as interesting, that
he had this conventional morality at that

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.520
point.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.470
He wanted it known that, now that he was caught,
he would do the right thing.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:49.750
So, this kind of moral schizophrenia is something
I tried to build into the characters."

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:50.830
Chainsperation

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:56.610
As for the movie's mood, Hooper and Henkel
had to look no further than the news to get

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:01.560
a read on the cultural unrest that existed
at the end of the Vietnam War.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:07.400
Meanwhile, the idea to implement a chainsaw
as the weapon of choice came from a particularly

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.190
terrible holiday shopping trip to a local
Montgomery Ward's.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:18.770
"I found myself in front of a chain saw display
in the hardware department, and I said 'If

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:26.080
I pick this damn thing up and start it, they'll
part like the red sea.'"

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.390
Surprise!

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:33.240
Hooper kept his Leatherface actor, Gunnar
Hansen, quarantined from the rest of the cast

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:39.870
until it was time for him to elicit some real
scares, so when he finally popped onto the

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.140
scene, he gave his co-stars quite a fright.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:48.570
It was just one of the many methods Hooper
would use to evoke honest tension from his

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.570
stars.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:53.590
He admitted in an interview to using a similar
tactic when it came to the wheelchair-bound

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:55.420
character Franklin, saying:

00:03:55.420 --> 00:04:00.240
"We wouldn't let Franklin have lunch with
the other actors, and we wouldn't let him

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.240
bathe."

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.690
Beef ... it's not what's for dinner

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:08.320
One of the central themes of the first Texas
Chain Saw movie was the way that the victims

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.710
were butchered in such similar ways to animals.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:16.550
Director Tobe Hooper, who himself had become
a vegetarian due to the perceived cruelty

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:22.610
of the meat industry, actually used the slaughterhouse
he'd pass by on regular drives as a set for

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:28.470
the film, because it played such a heavy part
in the philosophical undertones of the pic.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.060
He explained in an interview:

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.449
"I had an experience in a restaurant one time
where there was a huge trolley with beef being

00:04:35.449 --> 00:04:40.469
carved up, and I just transposed different
images onto it.

00:04:40.469 --> 00:04:45.550
Like, what if there was a nice little cow
there with a bowtie and a knife carving up

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:46.819
humans?

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:48.129
… It always disturbed me.

00:04:48.129 --> 00:04:51.189
It became part of the psychology of the film."

00:04:51.189 --> 00:04:56.919
He later admitted that he "thought the heart
of the film was about meat," even though the

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:01.420
characters' cannibalism isn't directly confirmed
in the original.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.420
Others agreed.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:07.550
In fact, the People for the Ethical Treatment
of Animals, better known as PETA, has given

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:14.330
the film the nod for being among the most
pro-veg flicks of all time, while fellow genre

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:19.639
auteur Guillermo del Toro went meat-free for
a while as a result of the movie.

00:05:19.639 --> 00:05:24.520
"From that moment until four years later,
I didn't eat any meat."

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.389
Chances seem pretty good del Toro didn't take
the time to see Sausage Party, huh?

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:31.810
"Oh, no me skin!

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.229
She's peelin' me skin!"

00:05:34.229 --> 00:05:35.229
"Aahhhh!"

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:36.659
Thanks for watching!

00:05:36.659 --> 00:05:40.860
